# PROJECT TITLE: LUDO MASTER — PREMIUM MULTIPLAYER BOARD GAME

## 1. GAME OVERVIEW AND MAIN OBJECTIVE

Create a complete, professional, visually attractive, highly engaging Ludo board game called **Ludo Master**. The game must support desktop computers, laptops, tablets, and mobile phones. It must provide a smooth experience for mouse users, touchscreen users, and players using different screen sizes.

The game should combine the familiar traditional Ludo experience with modern graphics, smooth animations, responsive controls, intelligent computer opponents, multiplayer gameplay, attractive sound effects, and an easy-to-understand interface.

The game must feel like a finished commercial-quality game rather than a basic board-game prototype.

The main objective is to move all four tokens of each player from their starting yard around the board and into their own home column. Players roll a six-sided dice, select eligible tokens, move according to the dice result, capture opponents when possible, and try to bring all four tokens safely into the final home area.

The first player to move all four tokens into their home wins the match.

Every game mechanic must function correctly. Dice rolls, token movement, safe squares, captures, extra turns, home entry, winning conditions, and player turns must follow consistent rules.

## 2. TARGET PLATFORMS AND RESPONSIVE DESIGN

Build the game for:

- Desktop computers and laptops.
- Android mobile phones.
- iPhones.
- Android and iPad tablets.
- Modern web browsers.
- Mouse, touch, and keyboard input where appropriate.

The layout must automatically adapt to the available screen dimensions.

### Desktop layout

On desktop screens, place the Ludo board in the center of the main game area. Use a balanced two-column or three-section layout where practical.

The central board should receive the greatest amount of visual attention.

Place player information around the board or in a clean surrounding panel. Show each player's color, remaining tokens, completed tokens, current turn, and relevant status.

Position the dice control near the active player's information. Keep turn history, sound controls, settings, and game actions accessible without covering the board.

Use comfortable spacing, readable typography, subtle shadows, rounded panels, and a consistent design system.

### Mobile layout

On mobile screens, prioritize the board and essential controls.

The board must scale proportionally and remain fully visible without unwanted horizontal scrolling. Use a square board container that preserves its aspect ratio.

Place the active player's information and dice button below the board or in a compact header, depending on the screen size.

Use touch targets large enough for comfortable interaction. Avoid tiny buttons, tightly packed controls, and accidental taps.

Allow vertical scrolling on small screens if required, but do not let decorative panels force the board outside the viewport.

The user must be able to roll the dice, select a token, inspect the current turn, and access the main menu using one hand on a typical mobile device.

### Tablet layout

Use a hybrid layout with a large centered board and compact player panels. Ensure that all four player colors and important game information remain readable.

### Responsive behavior

- Recalculate board size when the viewport changes.
- Preserve token positions and game state during resizing.
- Never reset an active match merely because the screen orientation changes.
- Avoid stretched circles, distorted dice, and misaligned grid cells.
- Support portrait and landscape layouts.
- Respect safe areas around device notches and rounded corners.
- Keep modals within the visible viewport.
- Prevent page scrolling or text selection during intentional touch-based token dragging.
- Use responsive CSS and a consistent layout system rather than hard-coded screen coordinates.

