## 7. LUDO BOARD AND MOVEMENT SYSTEM

Build the board using a well-defined logical grid and a predefined route for each player.

Every token should have an explicit state.

Possible token states:

1. In the starting yard.
2. On the main movement track.
3. On the player's private home path.
4. Finished inside the final home area.

Each token must have a unique identifier and belong to exactly one player.

### Starting yard

Each player begins with four tokens in their own colored starting yard.

Tokens in the starting yard cannot move along the track until the player rolls the required number, normally a six.

When a six is rolled, the player may bring an eligible token onto their starting square.

The newly deployed token must occupy the correct starting position and become part of the normal movement system.

A newly deployed token must not also move six additional spaces from the starting square unless an explicitly selected variant permits it.

### Main track

Once deployed, a token follows the player's predefined route around the board.

Movement must proceed one cell at a time in the correct direction.

The token must never jump to a visually adjacent but logically incorrect cell.

For each move, calculate the destination from the token's current route index and the dice result.

Animate the movement through the intermediate cells so the player can follow the journey.

### Home column

After completing the required circuit, the token enters its own colored home column.

Only the appropriate player's tokens may use that private home path.

Opponents must not capture tokens inside a protected home column.

### Final home

A token must reach the final home position using the correct number.

If the remaining distance to the finish is less than the rolled number, the token is not eligible to move under the exact-roll rule.

If no eligible token exists, the game must handle the turn according to the configured rules.

## 8. DICE SYSTEM

Create a six-sided dice with faces numbered one through six.

The dice should be visually attractive and easy to understand.

When the player rolls, animate the dice briefly with a rotation, bounce, or face-changing effect. Then reveal the final result clearly.

The final result must come from a valid random number generator or a secure server-authoritative system in online mode.

The visual animation must never change the actual result.

### Dice interaction

Players may roll the dice only when it is their turn and the game is waiting for a roll.

After a roll, disable the dice control until the player completes the required action or the turn advances.

If the player has legal moves, highlight the eligible tokens.

If no legal moves exist, display a short explanation and advance the turn according to the rules.

### Rolling a six

A six should allow the player to deploy a token from the yard when possible.

If a token can already move, the player may choose between deploying a new token and moving an eligible token.

Under the standard rule configuration, rolling a six grants another roll after the move is completed.

If the player rolls a six three consecutive times, apply the selected rule variant. A recommended traditional rule is that the third consecutive six forfeits the turn and cancels the third roll's movement.

Make this behavior configurable in the rules screen because house rules differ.

### Dice fairness

Do not use predictable visual animation values as the actual dice outcome.

Separate the dice result from the dice animation.

For local gameplay, use a suitable pseudorandom number generator. For competitive online gameplay, use server-side validation and authoritative turn processing.

