If multiple tokens are legal, do not move one automatically unless the game mode explicitly uses automatic move selection.

## 10. CAPTURING OPPONENT TOKENS

Capturing is an important part of the game.

When a player's token lands on a square occupied by one or more vulnerable opponent tokens, capture them according to the configured rules.

Captured tokens return to their respective starting yards.

### Capture rules

- Only resolve captures after a valid move is completed.
- Never capture a token on a designated safe square.
- Never capture a token inside its protected home column.
- Apply the same rules to human and computer players.
- Handle overlapping tokens deterministically.
- Update token counts, player status, and turn information after the capture.

Use a short capture animation. For example, the captured token can briefly shrink, flash, or move toward its home yard before returning to the starting position.

Display a concise message such as “Token captured!” or “Back to start!”

When the capture grants an extra turn under the configured rules, communicate this clearly.

## 11. SAFE SQUARES AND STARTING SQUARES

Clearly mark safe squares using a star, shield, or distinctive symbol.

Safe squares protect tokens from capture according to the selected ruleset.

Starting squares should also be visually identifiable. If the rules treat them as safe, encode that behavior explicitly rather than assuming every starting square is safe.

The interface must communicate safe-square behavior without overcrowding the board.

If a player lands on a safe square, show a subtle shield or protective animation.

Ensure that the board artwork and game logic agree on every safe square.

## 12. EXTRA TURNS AND TURN MANAGEMENT

The turn system must be predictable and consistent.

At all times, the game must know:

- Which player is active.
- Whether the player is waiting to roll.
- Whether a dice result is available.
- Which tokens are legally movable.
- Whether an animation or move is being processed.
- Whether an extra roll is permitted.
- Whether the match has ended.

Under the recommended standard rules, an extra turn may be granted for rolling a six, capturing an opponent, or reaching the final home position, depending on the selected ruleset.

These rewards must be configurable because not every Ludo variant uses the same extra-turn rules.

Do not grant duplicate extra turns accidentally when a move both captures an opponent and reaches a special destination.

Define an explicit rule for combining turn rewards. A recommended approach is to grant one additional turn for the completed move unless the selected variant specifies otherwise.

The game must never allow two players to act simultaneously in local mode.

